\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Vision Transformer architecture. The image is split into fixed-size patches, linearly embedded with position encodings, and processed by a standard Transformer encoder. A learnable classification token aggregates information for the final prediction. Figure inspired by Dosovitskiy et al. (2021).}}{25}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Training and validation curves showing loss and accuracy progression over 10 epochs for all three models. Note the rapid convergence and stable training dynamics.}}{33}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrices for slice-level tumor detection on test set (187 patients, $\sim $29,000 slices). All models show balanced performance with high accuracy and F1-scores, demonstrating effective tumor/non-tumor classification.}}{36}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces EfficientNet-B2 Grad-CAM visualizations. Heatmaps cover the tumor region but also show activation in surrounding brain tissue, which is typical of CNN-based models that learn texture and contextual features.}}{39}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces ResNet-50 Grad-CAM visualizations. Similar to EfficientNet-B2, heatmaps cover the tumor but activate broadly over surrounding tissue, consistent with CNN texture and context-based feature learning.}}{41}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces DeiT-Base Grad-CAM visualizations. The multi-head self-attention mechanism allows the model to compare all image patches at once, resulting in heatmaps that are tightly focused on the tumor region with minimal activation elsewhere.}}{43}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM visualization on a true negative (tumor-free) slice. The heatmap shows diffuse, low-intensity activation without localized attention, contrasting with tumor-positive slices where attention concentrates on the tumor region.}}{44}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Direct comparison of Grad-CAM attention patterns on the same slice across all three models. This enables direct visual comparison of how each architecture localizes tumor features.}}{45}{figure.caption.16}%
